MAWIS: A Cognitive Hydric State Operating System for Morocco

Toward a National Adaptive Water Intelligence Architecture for Semi-Arid Statess

Ouadi Maakoul | Avril 2026

---

## Abstract

Morocco faces structural hydric collapse — not a temporary drought emergency but a permanent transition to a lower-water equilibrium driven by aquifer liquidation, export agriculture paradoxes, governance fragmentation, and accelerating climate aridification. Existing responses are infrastructure-heavy and institutionally fragmented, lacking the predictive, adaptive, and incentive-aware architecture required for durable water sovereignty.

This dissertation introduces MAWIS — Morocco Adaptive Water Intelligence System — a six-layer Cognitive Hydric State Operating System that integrates satellite-based aquifer intelligence, multi-horizon drought forecasting, AI-driven agricultural allocation, renewable-synchronized desalination optimization, institutional governance redesign, and — critically — a transition and incentive architecture (MAWIS-0) that bridges current fragmented governance toward full national deployment without political shock.

The central thesis is that water governance failure in semi-arid states is not primarily a technology gap or an information gap. It is an **incentive-authority-intelligence alignment failure**. Formally:

**Governance Effectiveness = Intelligence × Incentives × Authority**

Current Moroccan water governance scores high on infrastructure investment, moderate on information production, and critically low on incentive alignment and integrated authority. MAWIS addresses all three dimensions simultaneously, proposing not merely a technical system but a **National Water Operating System** — the cognitive backbone of hydric sovereignty.

