## Chapter 2: Literature Review and State of the Art

### 2.1 Remote Sensing and Aquifer Intelligence

The GRACE-FO satellite mission enables regional-scale estimation of terrestrial water storage, including groundwater, by detecting subtle changes in Earth's gravity field. Combined with land cover analysis, GRACE data can characterize aquifer depletion trends across semi-arid zones. [MDPI](https://www.mdpi.com/2073-4441/17/21/3125)

Machine learning models — Decision Trees, Support Vector Machines, and Random Forests — applied to GRACE satellite data have demonstrated accurate prediction of groundwater level fluctuations in arid and semi-arid regions. [SciOpen](https://www.sciopen.com/article/10.26599/JGSE.2025.9280035) However, GRACE resolution (approximately 300 km) is insufficient for aquifer-level management. The scientific gap is the downscaling problem: translating basin-scale GRACE signals to aquifer-scale management decisions.

Recent work addresses this through data fusion: combining GRACE gravity anomalies with InSAR surface deformation measurements and in-situ piezometric data to reconstruct aquifer state at management-relevant spatial resolution. This three-layer fusion architecture is the sensing foundation of MAWIS Module 1.

### 2.2 Digital Twins and Smart Irrigation

Digital twins simulate real-time agricultural environments, enabling precise resource allocation, predictive maintenance, and scenario planning. AI enhances digital twin performance through machine learning and data-driven insights. When combined with ML, digital twins can significantly enhance agricultural productivity by improving decision accuracy and reducing resource wastage including water, fertilizers, and pesticides. [MDPI](https://www.mdpi.com/2076-3417/15/8/4228)

Smart irrigation systems combining automation, sensors, and data analytics can save 30–40% of water while maintaining crop performance. [Cherrytimes](https://italianberry.it/en/news/smart-irrigation-morocco-digital-agriculture-sustainable-farming-precision-irrigation-berries) Morocco's existing Digital Oasis program has combined satellite imagery with farmer-facing mobile apps to offer localized irrigation alerts and drought risk warnings. [Fanack Water](https://water.fanack.com/ai-smart-irrigation-mena/)

The research gap here is not the technology — it is the **scaling and incentive architecture**. Pilot programs demonstrate technical feasibility. National deployment requires a structured incentive mechanism that makes adoption economically rational for individual farmers, which current programs lack.

### 2.3 Water Governance and Institutional Design

The literature on water governance in semi-arid states consistently identifies three failure modes: information fragmentation, misaligned incentives, and authority dispersion. A critical paradox emerges across the MENA region: despite world-class remote sensing infrastructure, operational application remains diagnostically fragmented, failing to translate technological capability into resilient water governance. [ScienceDirect](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2665972726001443)

This dissertation's contribution to the governance literature is the formalization of the Intelligence × Incentives × Authority governance equation and the design of the MAWIS-0 transition layer — a phased migration architecture that addresses institutional resistance explicitly rather than assuming it away.

