## Chapter 6: Layer 3 — Smart Agricultural Water Allocation System (SAWAS)

### 6.1 Design Principle

Agriculture consumes approximately 85% of Morocco's freshwater. This is where the water crisis is won or lost. SAWAS is a two-level optimization hierarchy that translates PDIE drought forecasts and AIP aquifer signals into basin-level water budgets and farm-level irrigation schedules.

### 6.2 Level 1 — Basin Allocation Optimizer

The basin allocation problem is a constrained multi-objective optimization:

**Maximize:** Σᵢ vᵢ · wᵢ (aggregate crop economic value, weighted by water use)

**Subject to:**
- Σᵢ wᵢ ≤ W_available(t) + W_desalination(t) — total withdrawal constraint
- wᵢ ≥ wᵢ_min for i ∈ food_security_crops — cereal and staple protection floor
- ΔA_j(t) ≥ −δ_max for all aquifer j — maximum drawdown rate constraint
- wᵢ_export ≤ f(Virtual_Water_Budget(t)) — export crop water ceiling

The virtual water export constraint is the novel element. It operationalizes the accounting principle that water embedded in export crops — avocados, tomatoes, citrus — must be charged against national hydric accounts and bounded by available surplus above food security and urban supply requirements.

### 6.3 Level 2 — Field Irrigation Digital Twin

Machine learning and digital twin technologies integrated in smart irrigation systems enhance water-use efficiency by continuously monitoring real-time field data — soil moisture, plant growth indicators, and weather variables — enabling dynamic irrigation adjustments that prevent water wastage and mitigate drought risks. [Taylor & Francis Online](https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/27525783.2025.2562418)

At farm level, SAWAS deploys a crop-specific Digital Twin that models:

- Soil Water Balance: θ(t+1) = θ(t) + I(t) − ET_c(t) − D(t), where θ is volumetric soil moisture, I is irrigation input, ET_c is crop evapotranspiration, and D is drainage
- Crop Stress Response: yield loss as a function of water deficit using FAO-56 Ky coefficients per crop type
- Irrigation Decision: minimize cumulative water use subject to yield loss ≤ tolerance threshold

Input signals: LoRaWAN soil moisture sensors, automatic weather station data, satellite-derived NDVI (crop health proxy), and PDIE short-range weather forecasts.

**Output:** Farm-level irrigation schedule updated every 48 hours, delivered to farmers via mobile app in Arabic and Amazigh. Basin-level seasonal water budget updated weekly.

